  "message": "Thanks for sticking with this—I know it’s frustrating. The good news is your signal and data are excellent now, so we’re down to MMS-specific causes. Based on what you found (no storage/media permission and APN previously missing MMSC), the next best step is to grant the messaging app’s storage/photos permission and re-test, then verify the MMSC is now present. I’ll also show how to quickly confirm if your phone is Android or iPhone so I can tailor steps precisely.",
  "why_this_next": "MMS needs your messaging app to access the photo/video file. If storage/photos access isn’t granted, sends can silently fail even when data is perfect. And since the APN was missing MMSC earlier, we should double-check it’s now populated after the reset.",
  "quick_phone_type_check": [
    "Do you see a Play Store app (Android) or an App Store app (iPhone) on your home screen?",
    "Or open Settings:",
    "- iPhone: Settings > General > About shows Model Name and Software Version.",
    "- Android: Settings > About phone shows Model and Android version."
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "1) Grant the messaging app storage/media (photos) permission",
      "instructions": [
        "On Android:",
        "- Settings > Apps > Messages (your default SMS app) > Permissions.",
        "- Set “Photos and videos” to Allow. If you see “Files and media” on older Android versions, set that to Allow.",
        "- Ensure “SMS” is Allowed (you said it is).",
        "- In App info > Mobile data & Wi‑Fi, make sure Background data is On.",
        "On iPhone:",
        "- Settings > Messages > make sure “MMS Messaging” is On.",
        "- Settings > Privacy & Security > Photos > Messages > select “All Photos”."
      ],
      "tool_equivalents_you_can_run": [
        "check_app_permissions(app_name=\"messaging\")",
        "grant_app_permission(app_name=\"messaging\", permission=\"storage\")"
      ],
      "then_test": "With Wi‑Fi still OFF and 5G showing, try sending a single small photo (under ~1 MB) to yourself or a different contact."
    },
    {
      "title": "2) Verify APN now has an MMSC URL",
      "instructions": [
        "On Android: Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > Mobile network > Access Point Names > tap your active APN and confirm there is an MMSC field with a URL filled in.",
        "On iPhone: Settings > Cellular > Cellular Data Network (if visible) and ensure MMS settings are present; if not visible on your carrier, that’s okay as long as the APN reset completed.",
        "If MMSC is still blank, reset APN to default again, then reboot."
      ],
      "tool_equivalent_you_can_run": "check_apn_settings()"
    },
    {
      "title": "3) Run an MMS capability check",
      "instructions": [
        "Run can_send_mms() on your phone if available and share the result with me."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "4) Re-test MMS with Wi‑Fi OFF",
      "instructions": [
        "Confirm the status bar shows 4G/5G.",
        "Send a small photo to yourself and to another contact.",
        "Tell me exactly what happens (e.g., spinning, sending, failed icon)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "If it still fails after permissions + MMSC verification",
      "instructions": [
        "Toggle Mobile Data OFF, wait 10 seconds, then ON again.",
        "Turn Airplane Mode ON for 30 seconds, then OFF (you did this earlier, but after permission changes it can help refresh).",
        "Reboot the device once more.",
        "If you use a physical SIM, reseat it (power off, remove and reinsert SIM, power on)."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "what_i_checked_on_our_side": {
    "line_status": "Active",
    "signal_and_data": "You now have strong 5G and fast browsing",
    "plan_usage": "8.7 GB used out of 15 GB; you are not out of data"
  },
  "questions": [
    "Do you have an App Store (iPhone) or Play Store (Android)?",
    "After granting storage/photos permission to the messaging app, did the MMS send?",
    "Does your APN now show an MMSC URL?",
    "What does can_send_mms() report, if you can run it?"
  ]
}

