messages.content:
{
  "message": "Good news: your account and line are active. “No Signal” after turning Airplane Mode OFF usually points to the phone not seeing a tower or the SIM, not a data loss issue on your device.",
  "reassurance": "None of the steps below will erase your photos, messages, or apps. We won’t reset anything that affects your data without your clear OK.",
  "my_thought_process": [
    "Your line 555-123-2002 is active, so this isn’t an account hold.",
    "Airplane Mode was ON; now radios are back on, but the phone still isn’t connecting to a tower (“No Signal”).",
    "Most likely causes: SIM not recognized/locked, temporary radio glitch, or a network preference set to an older mode."
  ],
  "do_these_steps_in_order": [
    {
      "step": "1) Turn Mobile Data ON",
      "why": "While calls don’t require mobile data, turning it ON helps the phone fully register on the network and removes the “data disabled” message.",
      "iphone": "Settings > Cellular (or Mobile Data) > toggle Mobile Data ON.",
      "android": "Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > Mobile network (or SIMs) > turn Mobile data ON."
    },
    {
      "step": "2) Check SIM status",
      "why": "If the SIM is missing/locked, the phone can’t connect to any tower.",
      "iphone": "Settings > Cellular. Check the SIM/line status. Note any message like “No SIM,” “SIM locked,” or a carrier name.",
      "android": "Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > SIMs (or SIM cards). Check if your SIM shows Active, No SIM, or SIM locked.",
      "what_to_tell_me": "Exactly what it shows (Active, No SIM, SIM locked, carrier name, etc.). If it says SIM locked, don’t guess a PIN—tell me and we’ll handle safely."
    },
    {
      "step": "3) If physical SIM, reseat it (safe and won’t erase data)",
      "how_to_tell": "In the SIM screen you’ll typically see “Physical SIM” or “eSIM.” If it’s physical: power the phone OFF, eject the SIM tray, reinsert the SIM carefully, then power ON.",
      "tip": "After boot, wait ~30–60 seconds to see if bars appear."
    },
    {
      "step": "4) Reboot the phone",
      "why": "Refreshes all radios and registrations.",
      "how": "Power it fully OFF, wait 10 seconds, then power ON. Check if bars or a 4G/LTE/5G label appears."
    },
    {
      "step": "5) Ensure modern network mode is selected",
      "why": "Being stuck on 2G/3G can prevent a proper connection.",
      "iphone": "Settings > Cellular > Voice & Data > select 4G or 5G (if available); enable LTE/VoLTE if you see that option.",
      "android": "Settings > Network & Internet > Mobile network > Preferred network type > choose 4G/5G (sometimes called LTE/5G or 4G/5G preferred)."
    },
    {
      "step": "6) Enable Wi‑Fi Calling (optional backup since you have Wi‑Fi)",
      "why": "Lets you place/receive calls over Wi‑Fi while we finish troubleshooting.",
      "iphone": "Settings > Cellular > Wi‑Fi Calling > turn ON.",
      "android": "Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > Mobile network (or SIM settings) > Wi‑Fi Calling > turn ON."
    },
    {
      "step": "7) Quick location check",
      "why": "If the signal is marginal indoors, moving by a window or stepping just outside can confirm if this is a local coverage dip."
    }
  ],
  "please_reply_with": [
    "What SIM status shows (Active / No SIM / SIM locked / carrier name).",
    "After these steps, do you see any bars or a network label (4G/LTE/5G)?",
    "Were you able to place a test call?",
    "If you’re not sure whether it’s iPhone or Android, tell me the phone brand on the device (Apple, Samsung, Google, Motorola, etc.)."
  ]
}

