messages.content:
{
  "message": "Thanks for sticking with me—totally understand the frustration. The good news is your line is active and your SIM shows as working, so we’re likely dealing with the phone not registering to a tower (radio/settings), not anything that would affect your photos or messages.",
  "reassurance": "Nothing we’ll do below erases your photos, messages, or apps. We’re only toggling connection settings and doing safe steps like a restart or (if applicable) reseating a SIM.",
  "my_thought_process": [
    "Airplane Mode is now OFF, but the phone still says No Signal.",
    "SIM status is Active, so the phone sees the SIM.",
    "Most likely causes now: network mode preference isn’t ideal, a temporary radio glitch that needs a reboot, or the SIM needs a reseat if it’s physical. We’ll also turn on Wi‑Fi Calling so you can make/receive calls over Wi‑Fi while we sort this out."
  ],
  "steps_to_try_now": [
    {
      "step": "1) Turn ON Wi‑Fi Calling (so you can make calls over Wi‑Fi immediately)",
      "iphone": "Settings > Cellular (or Mobile Data) > Wi‑Fi Calling > turn ON.",
      "android": "Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > Mobile network/SIM settings > Wi‑Fi Calling > turn ON.",
      "notes": "This won’t erase anything. It just uses your Wi‑Fi for calls/texts while we fix cellular."
    },
    {
      "step": "2) Double‑check Mobile Data switch",
      "why": "You saw a “Data Disabled” note. Let’s force a refresh.",
      "how": "Go to Settings > Mobile network (or Cellular). Turn Mobile Data OFF, wait 10 seconds, then turn it ON again."
    },
    {
      "step": "3) Make sure network mode prefers 4G/5G",
      "why": "Being stuck on a legacy mode can block registration.",
      "iphone": "Settings > Cellular > Voice & Data > select 4G or 5G (if available); enable LTE/VoLTE if shown.",
      "android": "Settings > Network & Internet > Mobile network > Preferred network type > choose 4G/5G (sometimes called LTE/5G or 4G/5G preferred)."
    },
    {
      "step": "4) Reboot the phone",
      "why": "Refreshes radios and registration.",
      "how": "Fully power OFF, wait 10 seconds, power ON. After boot, wait ~30–60 seconds to see if bars or 4G/LTE/5G appear."
    },
    {
      "step": "5) If you have a physical SIM, reseat it (safe)",
      "how_to_tell": "In the SIM settings screen, does it mention “Physical SIM” or show a SIM tray on your device? If you’re unsure, tell me what you saw there.",
      "how": "Power OFF the phone. Eject the SIM tray, remove and reinsert the SIM carefully, then power ON. Wait ~30–60 seconds and check the top of the screen."
    },
    {
      "step": "6) Quick location check",
      "why": "If signal is marginal indoors, moving next to a window or stepping outside for a moment can confirm if this is a local coverage dip."
    }
  ],
  "optional_next_if_still_no_signal": [
    "We can reset your mobile network’s APN settings to default and then reboot. This does not delete personal data. Let me know if you want to do this and I’ll guide you."
  ],
  "please_reply_with": [
    "Did Wi‑Fi Calling turn ON successfully? Can you place a call over Wi‑Fi?",
    "After steps 2–4, what does the top of the screen show now (any bars or 4G/LTE/5G)?",
    "Does your SIM settings screen say eSIM or Physical SIM? If physical, were you able to reseat it?",
    "If you know the phone brand (Apple, Samsung, Google, Motorola, etc.), tell me—that helps me tailor the exact menu names."
  ]
}

