messages.content:
{
  "summary": "Seeing “No Signal” right after turning off Airplane Mode can be normal for a short time while the phone reconnects. Let’s do a few safe, reversible steps to register on a network in France. I’ll explain why each step helps.",
  "thought_process": "Airplane Mode was just turned off. Your phone now needs to register with a partner network. If it doesn’t quickly, the usual causes are a temporary registration delay, signal conditions (indoors), SIM not being recognized, or a limiting network mode setting. We’ll start with the quickest, safest checks.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Wait up to 60 seconds",
      "why": "Sometimes it takes a bit for the phone to find and register on a roaming network.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Watch the top bar for up to a minute and see if any bars or a label like 4G/LTE/5G/3G appears."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Quick refresh of the radios",
      "why": "Forces a clean re‑search for networks.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Swipe down twice to open Quick Settings.",
        "Tap Airplane Mode ON, wait 10 seconds, then tap it OFF.",
        "Wait another 20–30 seconds and check the top bar again."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check SIM status (read‑only)",
      "why": "Confirms your phone sees the SIM properly when roaming.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Open Settings > About phone (or About device).",
        "Tap Status > SIM status (names can vary slightly).",
        "Tell me what it shows for: SIM status (Active/Ready, Missing, or Locked) and Network (does it list a carrier like Orange/SFR/Bouygues or say ‘Not available’?)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Ensure Mobile Data and Data Roaming are ON",
      "why": "MMS uses mobile data, and while roaming you need the Data Roaming switch ON. These won’t delete anything and can be turned off later.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > Mobile network (or SIMs).",
        "Turn ON Mobile data.",
        "Turn ON Data roaming."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Set Preferred Network Type to 4G/5G (auto)",
      "why": "Avoids getting stuck on older modes that may not register well abroad.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Settings > Network & Internet > Mobile network > Preferred network type.",
        "Choose an option like 5G/4G/3G (Auto) or 4G/3G (Auto) if 5G isn’t available."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Move briefly near a window or outdoors",
      "why": "Buildings can block signal, especially during initial registration.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Stand near a window or step outside for a moment and watch the top bar for bars and a network label."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Restart the phone (only if still no signal)",
      "why": "A reboot refreshes all radio services and often completes roaming registration.",
      "what_to_do": [
        "Hold the power button, choose Restart (or Power off, wait 10 seconds, then power on).",
        "After it boots, wait 30–60 seconds and check for bars and 4G/LTE/5G/3G."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "what_success_looks_like": [
    "Airplane icon is gone (you already have this).",
    "You see 1+ signal bars.",
    "A network label appears (5G, LTE/4G, or 3G).",
    "You may see an ‘R’ for roaming."
  ],
  "after_signal_returns": [
    "Briefly turn off Wi‑Fi and try loading a website to confirm data works.",
    "Then send yourself a picture message (MMS) to test."
  ],
  "questions": [
    "After steps 1–2, do you now see any bars or a network label?",
    "What does SIM status show (Active/Ready, Missing, or Locked) and does it list a network?",
    "Are Mobile data and Data roaming both ON now?",
    "Would you like me to guide you through the exact menu names on your specific Android brand (Samsung/Pixel/OnePlus/etc.)?"
  ],
  "reassurance": "All steps above are safe and reversible. They won’t delete any photos or messages. I’ll stay with you and adjust the plan based on what you see on screen."
}

